n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{{n}^{2}+6n+8}.$\n\n*Hint: Try to factor the denominator first.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101547 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ec8e0245-c575-5c1a-8f05-28e1a7438098" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101547 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that the series can be written as\n\n$$\n8{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}+6n+8}.\n$$\n\nWe can factor the denominator and use partial fractions to show that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}+6n+8}=\\frac{1}{(n+2)(n+4)}=\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{n+2}-\\frac{1}{n+4}).\n$$\n\nSo, our series can be written as\n\n$$\n8{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{n+2}-\\frac{1}{n+4})=4{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{n+2}-\\frac{1}{n+4}).\n$$\n\nLet's now compute the first few partial sums:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{2} & =4[(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{6})] \\\\ {s}_{3} & =4[(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{6})+(\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{7})] \\\\ {s}_{4} & =4[(\\frac{1}{4}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{7})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\frac{1}{8})] \\\\ {s}_{5} & =4[(\\frac{1}{4}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\frac{1}{5}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\frac{1}{8})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}}-\\frac{1}{9})] \\\\ {s}_{6} & =4[(\\frac{1}{4}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\frac{1}{5}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}}-\\frac{1}{9})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}}-\\frac{1}{10})]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe notice that all of the terms cancel except $\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{5},$ and the $-\\frac{1}{N+3}$ and  $-\\frac{1}{N+4}$ terms. Therefore, the $N$th partial sum is\n\n$$\n{s}_{N}=4[\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{N+3}-\\frac{1}{N+4}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we can determine the sum of the series by taking the limit of ${s}_{N}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{{n}^{2}+6n+8} & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }⁡{s}_{N} \\\\  & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }⁡4[\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{N+3}-\\frac{1}{N+4}] \\\\  & =4{lim}_{N\\to \\infty }⁡[\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{N+3}-\\frac{1}{N+4}] \\\\  & =4[\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{5}-0-0] \\\\  & =4[\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{5}] \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{9}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101819 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101819 #edb/ref 1061 643686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101819 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f09ad925-45f1-583f-9d96-f7db4c4c6d03" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101819 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186102012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101820 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101820 #edb/ref 1061 643687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101820 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1d7a95a6-44a2-55cb-a4bc-c0ee15b8cece" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101820 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101821 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101821 #edb/ref 1061 643690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101821 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "053b72df-f735-53b4-9d30-87093c9b677a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101821 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101822 #edb/ref 1061 643694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101822 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "aaec4c89-d6c7-590f-98d3-134abf397033" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101822 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102012 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's see a trick that can be used to compute the sum of the following series:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n = 1}^\\infty \\dfrac 1 {n (n + 1)}\n$$\n\nFirst, we can use partial fractions to write\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{n(n+1)} = \\dfrac{1}{n} - \\dfrac{1}{n+1}.\n$$\n\nThen the sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n = 1}^\\infty \\dfrac 1 {n (n + 1)} = \\sum_{n = 1}^\\infty\\left(\\dfrac{1}{n} - \\dfrac{1}{n+1}\\right).\n$$\n\nComputing the first few partial sums, *without* simplifying, notice that many terms cancel:\n\n$\\require{cancel}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{1} & =(1\\,-\\,\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ {s}_{2} & =(1-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{2}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{2}}\\,-\\,\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ {s}_{3} & =(1-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{2}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{2}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}}\\,-\\,\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\ {s}_{4} & =(1-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{2}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{2}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}}\\,-\\,\\frac{1}{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor each partial sum, we always end up canceling all terms except for the first and last terms. We say that this infinite series is a **telescoping series**, meaning that it consists of only a finite number of terms after canceling. \n\nSo, if $s_N$ is the $N$th partial sum of the series, we have\n\n$$\ns_N \t= {\\color{blue}{1}} {\\color{red}{\\,-\\, \\dfrac 1 {N + 1}}} .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can determine the sum of the series by computing the limit of $s_N$ as $N\\to\\infty.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n(n+1)} & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }(1-\\frac{1}{N+1}) \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102012 #edb/ref 1065 6768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102012 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7785a1f9-f6fd-5dac-bbab-9da2b149e341" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102012 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533137 #edb/ref 1080 "The series is divergent" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533137 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533137 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0319b517-5a89-537a-bad6-f642b5dabd4e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533139 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533139 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533139 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0356df3f-742c-5b7e-8638-24299c2342cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533148 #edb/ref 1080 "The series is divergent" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533148 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499