cel{\\frac{1}{3}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}}-\\frac{1}{6})].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that for each partial sum, we always end up canceling all terms with the exception of the first and last terms. Therefore, the $N$th partial sum is\n\n$$\n{s}_{N}=\\frac{1}{4}[\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{N+1}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we can determine the sum of the series by taking the limit of ${s}_{N}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4n(n+1)} & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }{s}_{N} \\\\  & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{4}[\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{N+1}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\frac{1}{2}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533979 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533979 #edb/ref 1069 "q-53562" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533979 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533979 #edb/ref 1071 "${\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{n(n+1)}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533979 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "14bccc9f-2da5-5aa2-aa0a-d74284957688" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533979 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that the series can be written as\n\n$$\n6{\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n(n+1)}.\n$$\n\nUsing partial fractions, we can write the series as\n\n$$\n6{\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{n}-\\frac{1}{n+1}).\n$$\n\nComputing the first few partial sums, starting with $n=3,$ gives\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{3} & =6[(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{4})] \\\\ {s}_{4} & =6[(\\frac{1}{3}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}}-\\frac{1}{5})] \\\\ {s}_{5} & =6[(\\frac{1}{3}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}}-\\frac{1}{6})] \\\\ {s}_{6} & =6[(\\frac{1}{3}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\frac{1}{7})].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in each partial sum, we always end up canceling all terms except for the first and last terms. Therefore, the $N$th partial sum is\n\n$$\n{s}_{N}=6[\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{N+1}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we can determine the sum of the series by taking the limit of ${s}_{N}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{n(n+1)} & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }{s}_{N} \\\\  & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }6[\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{N+1}] \\\\  & =6[\\frac{1}{3}-0] \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533981 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533981 #edb/ref 1069 "q-124095" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533981 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533981 #edb/ref 1071 "${\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{3n(n+1)}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533981 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "183b979f-8612-5577-8e86-5be342266cb9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533981 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that the series can be written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{3}{\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n(n+1)}.\n$$\n\nUsing partial fractions we can write the series as\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{3}{\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{n}-\\frac{1}{n+1}).\n$$\n\nComputing the first few partial sums, starting with $n=5,$ gives\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{5} & =\\frac{4}{3}[(\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{6})] \\\\ {s}_{6} & =\\frac{4}{3}[(\\frac{1}{5}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\frac{1}{7})] \\\\ {s}_{7} & =\\frac{4}{3}[(\\frac{1}{5}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}}-\\frac{1}{8})] \\\\ {s}_{8} & =\\frac{4}{3}[(\\frac{1}{5}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}}-\\frac{1}{9})].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that for each partial sum, we always end up canceling all terms with the exception of the first and last terms. Therefore, the $N$th partial sum is\n\n$$\n{s}_{N}=\\frac{4}{3}[\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{N+1}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we can determine the sum of the series by taking the limit of ${s}_{N}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{3n(n+1)} & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }{s}_{N} \\\\  & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }\\frac{4}{3}[\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{N+1}] \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}[\\frac{1}{5}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533995 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533995 #edb/ref 1069 "q-352359" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533995 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533995 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the series\n${\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{(n+3)(n+4)}.$\nDo not round the answer.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $\\frac{1}{(n+3)(n+4)}=\\frac{1}{n+3}-\\frac{1}{n+4}.$*\n\n${\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{(n+3)(n+4)}=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533995 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3aca4a3b-d87a-5bf6-923c-3d648e553340" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533995 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that the series can be written as\n\n$$\n2{\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+3)(n+4)}\n$$\n\nWe are also given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(n+3)(n+4)}=\\frac{1}{n+3}-\\frac{1}{n+4}.\n$$\n\nSo, our series can be written as\n\n$$\n2{\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{n+3}-\\frac{1}{n+4}).\n$$\n\nLet's now compute the first few partial sums:\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{3} & =2[(\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{7})] \\\\ {s}_{4} & =2[(\\frac{1}{6}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}}-\\frac{1}{8})] \\\\ {s}_{5} & =2[(\\frac{1}{6}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}}-\\frac{1}{9})] \\\\ {s}_{6} & =2[(\\frac{1}{6}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{9}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{9}}-\\frac{1}{10})]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in each partial sum, we always end up canceling all terms except for the first and last terms. Therefore, the $N$th partial sum is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{N} & =2(\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{N+4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can determine the sum of the series by taking the limit of ${s}_{N}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{(n+3)(n+4)} & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }{s}_{N} \\\\  & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }2(\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{N+4}) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{6}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534009 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534009 #edb/ref 1069 "q-352380" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534009 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534009 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the series\n${\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}-6n+8}.$\nDo not round the answer.\n\n*Hint: Try to factor the denominator first.*\n\n${\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}-6n+8}=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534009 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5580c754-8067-5747-aaed-12ae4add5b01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534009 #edb/ref 1074 "We can factor the denominator and use partial fractions to show that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}-6n+8}=\\frac{1}{(n-4)(n-2)}=\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{n-4}-\\frac{1}{n-2}).\n$$\n\nSo, our series can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{n-4}-\\frac{1}{n-2})=2{\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{n-4}-\\frac{1}{n-2}).\n$$\n\nLet's now compute the first few partial sums:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{5} & =2[(1-\\frac{1}{3})] \\\\ {s}_{6} & =2[(1-\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4})] \\\\ {s}_{7} & =2[(1-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}})+(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}}-\\frac{1}{5})] \\\\ {s}_{8} & =2[(1-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}})+(\\frac{1}{2}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}}-\\frac{1}{5})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}}-\\frac{1}{6})]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe notice that all of the terms cancel except $1,\\frac{1}{2},$ and the $-\\frac{1}{N-3}$ and  $-\\frac{1}{N-2}$ terms. Therefore, the $N$th partial sum is\n\n$$\n{s}_{N}=2(1+\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{N-3}-\\frac{1}{N-2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, we can determine the sum of the series by taking the limit of ${s}_{N}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}-6n+8} & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }{s}_{N} \\\\  & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }2(1+\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{N-3}-\\frac{1}{N-2}) \\\\  & =2(1+\\frac{1}{2}-0-0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534016 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534016 #edb/ref 1069 "q-124562" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534016 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534016 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate \n${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{(n+3)(n+5)}.$\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $\\frac{1}{(n+3)(n+5)}=\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{n+3}-\\frac{1}{n+5}).$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534016 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6605d276-0c90-5d53-af14-89557fa03206" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534016 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that the series can be written as\n\n$$\n12{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+3)(n+5)}.\n$$\n\nWe are also given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(n+3)(n+5)}=\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{n+3}-\\frac{1}{n+5}).\n$$\n\nSo, our series can be written as\n\n$$\n12{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{n+3}-\\frac{1}{n+5})=6{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{n+3}-\\frac{1}{n+5}).\n$$\n\nLet's now compute the first few partial sums:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{0} & =6[(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{5})] \\\\ {s}_{1} & =6[(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{5})+(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{6})] \\\\ {s}_{2} & =6[(\\frac{1}{3}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})+(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{6})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}}-\\frac{1}{7})] \\\\ {s}_{3} & =6[(\\frac{1}{3}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})+(\\frac{1}{4}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}}-\\frac{1}{7})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\frac{1}{8})] \\\\ {s}_{4} & =6[(\\frac{1}{3}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})+(\\frac{1}{4}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\frac{1}{8})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}}-\\frac{1}{9})]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe notice that all of the terms cancel except $\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{1}{4},$ and the $-\\frac{1}{N+4}$ and  $-\\frac{1}{N+5}$ terms. Therefore, the $N$th partial sum is\n\n$$\n{s}_{N}=6[\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{N+4}-\\frac{1}{N+5}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we can determine the sum of the series by taking the limit of ${s}_{N}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{(n+3)(n+5)} & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }{s}_{N} \\\\  & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }6[\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{N+4}-\\frac{1}{N+5}] \\\\  & =6{lim}_{N\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{N+4}-\\frac{1}{N+5}] \\\\  & =6[\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{4}-0-0] \\\\  & =6[\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{4}] \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{7}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534027 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534027 #edb/ref 1069 "q-352362" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534027 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534027 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=7}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(n-3)(n-2)}=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534027 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "810ad1b2-7d88-5360-b92e-a06b02c3201b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534027 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we note that using partial fractions, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(n-3)(n-2)}=\\frac{1}{n-3}-\\frac{1}{n-2}.\n$$\n\nSo, our series can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=7}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{n-3}-\\frac{1}{n-2}).\n$$\n\nLet's now compute the first few partial sums:\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{7} & =(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\ {s}_{8} & =(\\frac{1}{4}-\\canc